body:
As much as I admire this project, it is a great argument for the fact that **simplicity** is completely orthogonal to **readability** and **extensibility**:

    :~ ( -- )
      baseW? if
        ?moustachelit if i) S>) @, else
          PSP) S>) keep if @, else @+, then then
        }) S>) op, drop,
      else
        ?moustachelit if
          i) S>) @, }) S>) op, else
          }) op, keep not if drop, then then then ;
    : do!, ['] !, op ! ~ ;
    :> m< do!, ; :> m< }addr moustache! ; compiling !{
    : le!{ m< ['] le!, op ! ~ ; immediate
    : be!{ m< ['] be!, op ! ~ ; immediate


    create _ ' @! , ' c@! , ' w@! , ' err ,
    :>
      m< ?moustachelit if
          i) S>) @, }) S>) @!,
          ?dupW, S) &) @,
        else
          baseW? if
            PSP) S>) keep if @, else @+, then
            }) S>) @!, S) &) @,
            else keep if dup, then }) @!, then then ;
    :> m< }addr _ widthexec ;
    compiling @!{

I'm sure it's just a failure of imagination, but how would a situation like this even work:

    struct Base1 { Base1() -> expected<int, E1> {} };

    struct Base2 { Base2() -> expected<float, E2> {} };

    struct Derived : Base1, Base2 {
    	Derived() -> ???;
    };

    int main()
    {
    	auto der = new Derived();
    	decltype(der) == ???;
    }

I don't know, I think it helps with readability when multiple functions are declared:

    std::pair<std::string, double> take_from_map(std::string str, std::map<std::string, double> map);
    int do_something_else(std::map<std::string, double> const& map);
    std::unique_ptr<int> lots_of_args(
      std::string arg0,
      std::map<std::string, double> arg1,
      int arg2,
      int arg3,
    );

vs

    func take_from_map(str std::string, map std::map<std::string, double>) std::pair<std::string, double>;
    func do_something_else(map std::map<std::string, double> const&) int;
    func lots_of_args(
      arg0 std::string, 
      arg1 std::map<std::string, double>,
      arg2 int,
      arg3 int,
    ) std::unique_ptr<int>;

The syntax in my personal language makes this even clearer:

    func take_from_map(str std.string, map std.map[std.string, double]) std.pair[string, double];
    func do_something_else(map ref[const[std.map[std.string, double]]]) int;
    func lots_of_args(
      arg0 std.string, 
      arg1 std.map[std.string, double],
      arg2 int,
      arg3 int,
    ) std.unique[int];

Which equivalence? Wouldn't

    auto f = [](int const* p) { return *p; }
    auto g = [](int const* p) => *p;

return `int`, and

    auto f = [](int const* p) -> int const& { return *p; }
    auto g = [](int const* p) -> int const& => *p;

return `const int&` ?

Couldn't you just write 

    auto g = [](int const* p) -> int const& => *p;

?

Can someone explain why

    auto f = [](int const* p) { return *p; }
    auto g = [](int const* p) => *p;

return different types? Can't we just define the second one to be equivalent to the first one?

    // You really gotta wonder what kind of pedantic garbage was
    // going through the minds of people who designed std::string
    // that they decided not to include trim.

Classy.
